{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ll rule for sorting whole map pairs by their compiled key/value content addresses, and set elements by their compiled content addresses, at the collection emitter. Explicit calls and vectors retain their order.\n\nOne limit needs to be explicit: this preserves the VM’s evaluation rules, but it cannot preserve every effect sequence produced by the old host-dependent traversal. The ruling will define the portable order and require tests for effects, failures, and keys that evaluate equal."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files test | rg '(frontends|frontend).*test'; sed -n '200,245p' test/yang/clojure_test.clj; sed -n '1,42p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc; sed -n '1147,1210p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; rg -n ':application|:operands' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc | head -12; rg -n 'c1-1|clojure-manifest|:segment/legacy' test/yang/frontend_test.cljc test/yin/repl/*test* | head -20; git log -1 --format='%h %s' 2c80a421; git log -1 --format='%h %s' fb493d52\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files test | rg '(frontends|frontend).*test'; sed -n '200,245p' test/yang/clojure_test.clj; sed -n '1,42p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc; sed -n '1147,1210p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; rg -n ':application|:operands' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc | head -12; rg -n 'c1-1|clojure-manifest|:segment/legacy' test/yang/frontend_test.cljc test/yin/repl/*test* | head -20; git log -1 --format='%h %s' 2c80a421; git log -1 --format='%h %s' fb493d52\"","aggregated_output":"test/yang/frontend_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/frontend_catalog_test.cljc\n(deftest test-dynamic-collection-literals\n  ;; unquoted collections evaluate their elements like Clojure; constant\n  ;; collections stay literal nodes; quoted collections stay data\n  (testing \"Constant collections stay literals\"\n    (is (= {:type :literal, :value [1 2]} (yang/compile '[1 2])))\n    (is (= {:type :literal, :value '(1 x 2)} (yang/compile '(quote (1 x 2))))))\n  (testing \"Dynamic vectors lower to conj over []\"\n    (let [ast (yang/compile '(let [x 7] [x 2]))]\n      (is (= :application (:type ast)))\n      (is (= 'conj (get-in ast [:operator :body :operator :name])))\n      (is (= {:type :literal, :value []} (first (get-in ast [:operator :body :operands])))))\n    (is (= [7 2] (compile-and-run '(let [x 7] [x 2])))))\n  (testing \"Dynamic sets lower to conj over #{}\"\n    (is (= #{7 2} (compile-and-run '(let [x 7] #{x 2})))))\n  (testing \"Dynamic maps evaluate keys and values\"\n    (is (= {7 8} (compile-and-run '(let [x 7] {x (+ x 1)})))\n        \"map keys are evaluated, not taken as data\"))\n  (testing \"Nested collections evaluate recursively\"\n    (is (= [7 [8] {:k #{9}}]\n           (compile-and-run '(let [x 7] [x [(+ x 1)] {:k #{(+ x 2)}}])))))\n  (testing \"Quoted dynamic collections remain data\"\n    (is (= '[x y] (compile-and-run '(quote [x y])))))\n  (testing \"Empty lists are self-evaluating data, constant or nested\"\n    (is (= [()] (compile-and-run '[()])))\n    (is (= [() 1] (compile-and-run '(let [x 1] [() x])))))\n  (testing \"Nested quote is stripped and the inner form evaluated\"\n    ;; (quote x) counts as constant only at top level; nested inside a\n    ;; collection it must reach compile-form so the quote is stripped\n    (is (= '[x] (compile-and-run (list 'let '[x 7] ['(quote x)]))))))\n\n\n(deftest test-compile-let\n  (testing \"Compiling let bindings\"\n    (testing \"Single binding\"\n      (let [ast (yang/compile '(let [x 1] x))]\n        (is (= :application (:type ast)))\n        (is (= :lambda (get-in ast [:operator :type])))\n        (is (= ['x] (get-in ast [:operator :params])))\n        (is (= [{:type :literal, :value 1}] (:operands ast)))))\n    (testing \"Multiple bindings\"\n      (let [ast (yang/compile '(let [x 1 y 2] (+ x y)))]\n        (is (= :application (:type ast)))\n        ;; Outer lambda for x\n        (is (= :lambda (get-in ast [:operator :type])))\n        (is (= ['x] (get-in ast [:operator :params])))\n        ;; Inner lambda for y\n(ns yin.vm.semantic.linearize\n  \"`project`: the Universal AST → the register-shaped canonical vector\n   (`docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.3).\n\n   One flattening, two lanes: the datom\n   lane reads `:yin/*` datoms through an entity index, the row lane reads\n   code-as-tuples rows (§5.1) by the grammar table's positions. Both hand\n   the same walk a node reader, so the order is shared by construction:\n   operator, then operands left to right; a conditional's test,\n   consequent, alternate; occurrences expanded positionally; saturated\n   defaults; lambda bodies out of line after the main sequence, in one\n   FIFO queue that bodies discovered inside bodies join at the back.\n\n   Registers are body-local and minted pre-order: a destination is minted\n   when its expression is entered, before its children; a conditional's\n   arms are lowered into the conditional's destination and mint nothing\n   themselves; a `:resume` mints like every other expression though no\n   instruction names its id as a destination. No moves, folds or\n   rewrites.\n\n   Provenance is a side table beside the vector, never inside it; the\n   code address is `ucf/code-address` of the vector.\"\n  (:require [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ucf :as ucf]))\n\n\n(def unsupported\n  \"Node types outside the shared supported AST vocabulary, rejected at\n   projection naming the node.\"\n  #{:yin/macro-expand :vm/store-update})\n\n\n(def ^:private slot-position\n  \"`[tag field]` → the field's row position (§2.5: id 0, tag 1, first\n   slot 2), from `vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar`. Provenance path steps\n   are these positions in both lanes.\"\n  (reduce-kv (fn [m tag slots]\n               (reduce (fn [m i]\n                         (assoc m [tag (first (nth slots i))] (+ 2 i)))\n                       m\n                       (range (count slots))))\n             {}\n                                     (same-content? e (some #(when (= e %) %) b)))\n                                   a))\n             (sequential? a) (and (sequential? b)\n                                  (= (count a) (count b))\n                                  (every? true? (map same-content? a b)))\n             :else (cbor/content= a b))))\n\n\n(defn ast->semantic-bytecode\n  \"Project a map AST to flat content-addressed rows (§6.1 codec boundary\n   projection). Returns `{:root row-id, :rows {row-id [row-id tag & slots]}}`.\n\n   Only the §2.3 slots of each node enter its row: `:eid`, `:macro?`,\n   `:yang/*` keys, non-`:application` `:tail?` marks, and any other key are\n   dropped. `sym`/`syms` slots lose all metadata; `data`/`key` payloads lose\n   reader positions at every depth and keep all other metadata. Defaults are\n   saturated per §2.4. Children are projected first, so a parent's body\n   commits to its children's ids; structurally identical subtrees mint one\n   id and are stored as one row (§4.4).\n\n   Two nodes that mint one id must have one content: the row is checked\n   against the stored row with `same-content?` (metadata at every depth,\n   leaves by `dao.jing.cbor/content=`), and a mismatch throws\n   \\\"Semantic bytecode address collision\\\" rather than letting one row silently\n   replace the other. No known input reaches that throw: the encoder hashes\n   symbol and collection metadata, and makes every NaN one quiet NaN, so two\n   NaN scalars are one row value. The guard is a defence against the encoder\n   and this check disagreeing.\"\n  [ast]\n  (let [rows (atom {})]\n    (letfn\n      [(slot-value\n         [tag node [field kind]]\n         (let [v (get node field)\n               v (if (nil? v) (get semantic-bytecode-defaults [tag field]) v)]\n           (case kind\n             :node (convert v)\n             :nodes (mapv convert v)\n             :sym (strip-symbol-meta v)\n             :syms (mapv strip-symbol-meta v)\n             :bool (boolean v)\n             (:data :key) (strip-reader-positions v)\n             v)))\n       (convert\n         [node]\n         (let [tag (:type node)\n               slots (or (get semantic-bytecode-grammar tag)\n                         (throw (ex-info \"Unknown AST node type\"\n                                         {:type tag, :node node})))\n               body (into [tag] (map #(slot-value tag node %)) slots)\n               id (jing/segment-key body)\n               row (into [id] body)\n               prior (get @rows id)]\n           (when (and prior\n                      (not (same-content? prior row)))\n             (throw (ex-info \"Semantic bytecode address collision\"\n                             {:id id, :row row, :prior prior})))\n           (swap! rows assoc id row)\n           id))]\n      (let [root (convert ast)]\n        {:root root, :rows @rows}))))\n\n\n(def ^:private source-position-keys\n311:                operands (:operands frame)\n329:                operands (:operands frame)\n351:                operands (:operands frame)\n493:        :application\n498:                       (second (:operands node))\n516:        (let [operands (or (:operands node) [])\n523:                         {:frame {:op op, :operands operands, :evaluated []},\n650:                  operands (:operands frame)\n696:                  operands (:operands frame)\n776:               :application\n2c80a421 Merge cc3-lowering-provenance: lowering provenance and the oracle C1 occurrence-key bridge\nfb493d52 docs(collab): archive the cc3 arc — rounds, reviews, notices — and the merge notices\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'c1-1|yang.clojure/c1|clojure-manifest' test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc test/yang/frontend_test.cljc test/yin/repl/frontend_catalog_test.cljc; sed -n '2920,2948p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '3080,3135p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc4/collab/yang-clojure/1795017275897-cc4-r2-evidence/cc4-r2-minimal-row-diff.edn\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'c1-1|yang.clojure/c1|clojure-manifest' test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc test/yang/frontend_test.cljc test/yin/repl/frontend_catalog_test.cljc; sed -n '2920,2948p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '3080,3135p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc4/collab/yang-clojure/1795017275897-cc4-r2-evidence/cc4-r2-minimal-row-diff.edn\"","aggregated_output":"test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc:2509:                    (repl-frontends/clojure-manifest)\n(defn compile\n  \"Main compiler entry point.\n\n  Compiles a Clojure form into Yin VM Universal AST.\n\n  Examples:\n    (compile 42)\n    => {:type :literal, :value 42}\n\n    (compile '(+ 1 2))\n    => {:type :application\n        :operator {:type :variable, :name +}\n        :operands [{:type :literal, :value 1}\n                   {:type :literal, :value 2}]}\n\n    (compile '(fn [x] (* x 2)))\n    => {:type :lambda\n        :params [x]\n        :body {:type :application\n               :operator {:type :variable, :name *}\n               :operands [{:type :variable, :name x}\n                          {:type :literal, :value 2}]}}\"\n  [form]\n  ;; the single-form entry runs the same reserved-prefix pre-pass as\n  ;; `compile-program`, so a REPL one-liner can no more spell a\n  ;; compiler-private (`dsg__`) name than a whole program can. Trusted\n  ;; compiler construction never reaches `compile`: it builds its own\n  ;; `dsg__` temporaries via `compile-form` directly.\n  (check-program-symbols! form)\n                  n)))\n            node (keys node))))\n\n\n(defn lower-unit\n  \"The `clj/run-main` entry's unit for the top-level source `forms`: one\n   thunk whose call runs the program — `forms` plus the definitions of\n   the guest library they need, compiled unmodified under `unit-opts`\n   and published to the runtime global namespace by\n   `publish-to-globals` — so the helper's export projection can read\n   each top-level definition. The two-argument form also takes the\n   projected `exports` (names the composition already bound in the\n   runtime global namespace): their free reads publish the same way, so\n   the projection is the caller's binding for them. The program's own\n   `yang.call` call sites need no free-name declaration: the convention\n   preamble `compile-program` emits defines those names locally, and\n   the donor publication pins the `yang.call` module for the qualified\n   reads that remain.\"\n  ([forms] (lower-unit forms []))\n  ([forms exports]\n   (let [source (vec forms)\n         library (library-for source)\n         program (compile-program (concat source library) true unit-opts)\n         macros (into #{}\n                      (comp (filter #(and (seq? %) (= 'defmacro (first %))))\n                            (map (comp strip-symbol-meta second)))\n                      (concat source library))\n         names (reduce disj\n                       (into (set (top-level-names source))\n                             (concat (map (comp strip-symbol-meta second) library)\n                                     (map symbol exports)))\n                       macros)]\n     ;; `publish-to-globals` rewrites nodes after `compile-program`\n     ;; finalized them, so the unit is finalized again over its\n     ;; finished tree: the rewrite's own applications are occurrences.\n     (annotate-call-sites (u/lam [] (publish-to-globals program names #{}))))))\n\n\n(defn output\n  \"The `clj/run-main` value as test data.\"\n  [value]\n  (pr-str value))\n:jvm [:segment/blake3-8598820bee9b622d827c7879729f60dad19244f9f9869ee693c1eaca58c1bb94 :application :segment/blake3-339d070ef739b0067484f7ff46930ab9e992cb3e4abadc27d403f294b8dea997 [:segment/blake3-201f235cbac998f43954454e9df0eb633717e79de535776f3f51e198a3759b10 :segment/blake3-81b62c1e0dbf783ce555376690c9cffdee2eaca50ff9da4247397a785d9340cf :segment/blake3-1db5dbd048522aa048759e5fb8ae47ccbf9e158906554b362080d77cf30a6178 :segment/blake3-8930bf5e7b03638896bc3bef03d4f1ae648e7460ed4b488bef2b9d5f619cbd90 :segment/blake3-e60543d220d61497d02184c1f7a087c15c3a20d0f305cae88647987f5ec99e3b :segment/blake3-1e03667ec9fa05b20761de3b84f623962235ff218fce26401bbcdd9d3aabeca8 :segment/blake3-3156a0708410c1e18d4eb15f567790a8c1ac683f219e720a847b0e21e54ca778] false]\n:dart [:segment/blake3-9a8f3a641f0e49b7381eccc74a1373b0d6bf97e954964587a449fcc2ee177e9b :application :segment/blake3-339d070ef739b0067484f7ff46930ab9e992cb3e4abadc27d403f294b8dea997 [:segment/blake3-201f235cbac998f43954454e9df0eb633717e79de535776f3f51e198a3759b10 :segment/blake3-81b62c1e0dbf783ce555376690c9cffdee2eaca50ff9da4247397a785d9340cf :segment/blake3-1db5dbd048522aa048759e5fb8ae47ccbf9e158906554b362080d77cf30a6178 :segment/blake3-1e03667ec9fa05b20761de3b84f623962235ff218fce26401bbcdd9d3aabeca8 :segment/blake3-3156a0708410c1e18d4eb15f567790a8c1ac683f219e720a847b0e21e54ca778 :segment/blake3-8930bf5e7b03638896bc3bef03d4f1ae648e7460ed4b488bef2b9d5f619cbd90 :segment/blake3-e60543d220d61497d02184c1f7a087c15c3a20d0f305cae88647987f5ec99e3b] false]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
