{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff --no-index --check /dev/null collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; wc -l -w collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; sed -n '20,82p' collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; sed -n '117,175p' collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff --no-index --check /dev/null collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; wc -l -w collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; sed -n '20,82p' collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; sed -n '117,175p' collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"     118    3235 collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n\n## RULING 1 — one content-canonical policy, at the collection emitter\n\n**MUST:** In `yang.clojure/compile-constructed-collection`, compile each map entry as a paired record containing its key AST and value AST, using the existing environment and macro environment and `tail? = false`. Recursively normalize any constructed collections in those children. Compute each child's canonical UAST root by the existing `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` projection. Sort the paired records in ascending `jing/canonical-compare` order of the two-element vector `[key-root value-root]`. Flatten the sorted records as `key-AST, value-AST, ...`, after the unchanged empty-map seed operand. Emit the existing `assoc` application with its existing operator resolution and parent tail mark.\n\nThis is option (a), specified precisely as **compiled-content address order**. It is not source-text order, host-map order, evaluated-key order, or a sort of the final side table. The comparator is the unsigned bytewise lexicographic order of Jing canonical bytes, with a proper prefix first (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:174–207`). The concrete key is the vector of two address values, not its printer rendering and not the hash of that vector. Key-root is primary, value-root is secondary. Do not independently sort keys and values.\n\n**MUST:** For a constructed set, use the same rule with each compiled element's canonical UAST root as the sort key; emit `conj` with the unchanged empty-set seed followed by those sorted element ASTs. For a vector, retain the supplied element order and do not sort.\n\nCompute each child AST and its sort key once per emitter invocation; sorting must not recompile or evaluate it. Keep the original AST, including its attached frontend/source metadata, with its paired record. The projected rows used to obtain a sort key are temporary compiler data, not published roots, origins, expansion events, or a new persisted ordering table. No new canonical field is needed.\n\nEqual sort keys mean equal executable content under the existing content-address integrity contract. Preserve every operand occurrence, including distinct source forms that compile to identical children; do not deduplicate them. A tie must not use host object identity, a counter, or printer output. Permuting truly identical compiled pairs/elements does not change canonical code or its C1 occurrence-key vector; their source metadata remains attached to the respective AST objects. Existing collision checks remain in force. This rule does not claim an ordering for arbitrary noncanonical host objects or extend the codec's supported domain.\n\n**Pipeline location:** this normalization is part of source/form-to-UAST lowering, inside the existing collection emitter (a private helper in the same file is allowed), after recursive child lowering and before assembling this constructor application's operand vector. It therefore precedes the owning final C1 path/root projection, macro traversal over the emitted tree, instruction lowering, and publication. Later compiler-generated form maps that re-enter `compile-form` pass through this same emitter. It is not a global post-expansion AST sorter. Existing finalizers still run after wrappers, tail marking, expansion, and other tree-changing passes at their established boundaries.\n\n`vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` hashes grammar slots, removes symbol-slot metadata, excludes frontend node keys, and retains semantic literal payload metadata (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1156–1210`). Using that projection avoids letting irrelevant reader metadata choose executable ordering. Generated naming remains the producer's responsibility: identical replay naming inputs must already yield identical child code. Do not mask a naming divergence by stripping generated names or origins.\n\nAlternatives considered:\n\n| Policy | Decision and reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| (a) Compiled-content address order at emitter | Adopted. One existing, typed, portable content identity works for mixed keys, arbitrary key expressions, nested collections, and generated form maps. It needs no reader or VM change. |\n| (b) Attach source-written order metadata | Rejected for this repair. The public form API receives maps/sets, and `arity-error-form` creates a map without a source-written sequence. Preserving order through readers, macro construction, and every rewriting producer would require a separate ordered-syntax contract. Source-position metadata cannot reconstruct order that was never retained. |\n| (c) Sort by key representation | Rejected if this means `pr-str`, `str`, keyword names, host comparison, or host hash. These either omit type/content distinctions or import host behavior. Sorting raw source forms by canonical bytes also lets non-executable source metadata influence order and bypasses the compiler's existing content projection. Compiled key-root ordering is the typed version selected above, with value-root as its explicit secondary key. |\n| (d) Patch only `arity-error-form`, or normalize only C1/hashes | Rejected. A hand-ordered arity constructor leaves every other dynamic map and set exposed. Ignoring operand order in hashes would identify executable programs with potentially different behavior. |\n\n## RULING 2 — evaluation semantics and applicability\n\n**MUST preserve:** the VM's application evaluation rule, operator lookup, seeds, key/value association, multiplicity, tail behavior, and normal exception/continuation behavior. On a successful construction every key and value is evaluated once; within a map pair the key precedes its value. Sets evaluate each retained element expression once even when evaluated values coincide. The constructor is invoked only after its operands finish, exactly as today. An exception or transfer of control during an operand prevents the later evaluations that the existing VM would not reach. Sorting is compile-time inspection of code, never evaluation of keys or values.\n\n**The order between pairs/elements is henceforth the canonical order in RULING 1.** It is executable order: the VM consumes that operand vector left to right. The frontend MUST NOT pretend this is an observationally invisible serialization change for every possible program. Effectful operands, competing failures, continuation capture, and distinct key expressions evaluating to an equal key can observe the selected order. For equal evaluated keys, ordinary `assoc`'s later-pair replacement behavior remains; which pair is later is now determined canonically. A shadowed constructor still receives the canonically ordered operands through the existing lookup rules.\n\nThere is an unavoidable boundary to “runtime evaluation semantics unchanged”: VM transition semantics and explicitly ordered syntax remain unchanged; an accidental old host traversal is not preserved as a portable language order. No one permutation can preserve two different old hosts' observable traces while also producing identical operand bytes. If “unchanged” means preserving each old host's trace for every effectful unordered form, that stronger requirement is incompatible with universal canonical reordering and is **not** a claim of this ruling. This ruling explicitly fixes the frontend's previously host-dependent choice and requires a frontend revision change. The arity-error map's unchanged built-in computation and distinct constant keys make that particular repair value-preserving.\n\nPrograms requiring a particular sequence must express it with existing ordered forms, for example `let` bindings that compute values in order followed by collection construction, or an explicit ordered constructor application. This round introduces no implicit purity inference, effect suppression, or new ordered-map metadata protocol.\n\n**Scope:** all dynamic/unquoted map and set forms reaching `compile-constructed-collection`, including compiler-generated maps, nested collections, and forms admitted through `compile-form`, `compile`, `compile-program` under native and argument-vector/unit options, and `lower-unit`/`library-for` consumers. The emitter branches covered are its map and set branches; the vector branch is an order-preservation control. `arity-error-form` is a covered producer, not a second ordering authority.\n\n`compile-literal` and `compile-quote` continue to emit constant/quoted collections as literal data. Their maps/sets already use Jing's unordered value encoding; do not turn them into calls. `compile-application`, `compile-dao-stream-apply-call`, `u/app`, lambda bodies, `let`, `do`, and pre-existing UAST operand vectors remain ordered and MUST NOT be sorted, including explicit `(assoc ...)` and `(conj ...)` calls. `u/sexp->uast` treats its non-list collection values as literals and is not a dynamic collection emitter. No universal “sort every assoc/conj application” transform is authorized. Other frontends are outside this implementation round; equivalent unordered-to-ordered emitters discovered elsewhere require their own bounded repair, not silent scope expansion.\n\n## RULING 3 — new fix-round bounds and acceptance contract\n\ncc4 r2 correctly stopped. Its forbidden-source rule remains true for that completed round. **A NEW compiler-determinism round is authorized by this contract to edit the following surfaces only; this findings session does not implement that round.** Coordinate shared-file ownership against the dispatch base before editing.\n\n| File/surface | Permitted change |\n| --- | --- |\n| `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` | Collection-emitter normalization, a private content-key helper if needed, and precise docstrings. No unrelated compiler refactor or exception rewrite. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc` | Only `clojure-manifest`'s compiler revision/lowering-profile declaration and associated description, per RULING 4. |\n| `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc` (new) | Portable regression/property and evaluation-order tests below. |\n| `test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc` | Preserve/import cc4's strengthened cohort; add focused regression assertions and replace active expectations only through the controlled re-pin below. |\n| `test/yang/clojure_test.clj`, `test/yang/frontend_test.cljc`, `test/yin/repl/frontend_catalog_test.cljc` | Focused collection regressions or manifest-revision expectations only where affected. |\n| `test/resources/yang/clojure/` | Versioned post-fix captures/expectations and historical copies needed to retain pre-fix evidence. |\n| `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj` | Manifest inclusion for the changed/new test namespaces only. The four unrelated missing namespaces remain a J2-owner dependency; no exclusions or bypasses. |\n| New files under `collab/yang-clojure/` | Fix findings, raw evidence, source/toolchain pins, before/after digest ledger, and budget continuation records. |\n\nAll other implementation files remain out of bounds: in particular Jing/CBOR, VM grammar and evaluators, `macro.cljc`, semantic lowering/derivers, encoder/loader machinery, shared core prelude, `dht.cljc`, and `slice9_site_tables.edn`. Do not repair this by changing hashing, C1 validation, origin formation, or the oracle. A needed change outside these bounds must be reported as a dependency rather than included incidentally.\n\n**Required tests (red before fix; all four hosts use the same recorded expectations):**\n\n1. **The reproduced arity-map regression.** Use the existing `argument-vector` and `lower-unit` replay members with their original source, options, and supplied coordinates. Assert the constructor row has the same ordered child-address vector on JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt; its paired operands obey RULING 1. Assert identical whole-tree canonical bytes/root, complete sorted occurrence-key vector, complete C1 relation, and each format's reconstructed instruction-provenance relation. Exercise named and anonymous arity errors, including anonymous source metadata, and verify the resulting arity payload values. The existing reproduced named case is the mandatory red; no special-case producer fix satisfies this test family.\n2. **Map representation/permutation property.** For a recorded corpus of distinct source-entry pairs, construct extensionally identical maps using insertion permutations and available ordinary array-map/hash-map representations. For sizes 0–4 enumerate all permutations; include sizes 8, 9, 16, and 32 with recorded forward, reverse, rotated, and fixed shuffled permutations so representation thresholds are crossed. Include constant keys/dynamic values, dynamic keys, heterogeneous scalar and quoted collection keys, nested maps/sets/vectors, floats, generated destructuring names, duplicate subtrees, and reader/non-reader metadata. At least one dynamic expression must reach the emitter in each nonconstant case. For fixed options and admission/expansion inputs, every permutation/representation on every host MUST yield identical canonical tree bytes/root, full C1/key vectors, and per-format provenance. Compare byte content, not host array identity or printed forms. Constant cases separately assert the literal fast path.\n3. **Set representation/permutation property.** The same bounded sizes/permutations for dynamic sets; require identical roots and full relations under a fixed replay. Include nested dynamic sets and different expressions evaluating to the same value. Assert element-address ordering and retain all expression occurrences before runtime set insertion. Do not deduplicate compiled elements as an optimization.\n4. **Order, pairing, and multiplicity oracle.** Independently project each compiled key/value child and assert that the actual emitted pair-key vector is sorted by the specified comparator, that pairs remain intact, and that the seed is first. Add different source key forms that compile to the same key code with different values to exercise the value-root secondary key; add distinct forms compiling to an identical entire pair/element to exercise ties without occurrence loss. Changing only source-position or variable-node metadata MUST NOT change code order/root. Preserve semantic metadata in literal payloads. Use an independent expectation builder, not the emitter helper being tested.\n\nAcceptance remains pending the new fix, independently checked re-pins, four-host regression results, legacy execution evidence, and the separate cc4 budget closure. This document rules policy and bounds; it does not claim those gates have passed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '80,118p' collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; git status --short -- collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc; shasum -a 256 src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '80,118p' collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md; git status --short -- collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc; shasum -a 256 src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"2. **Map representation/permutation property.** For a recorded corpus of distinct source-entry pairs, construct extensionally identical maps using insertion permutations and available ordinary array-map/hash-map representations. For sizes 0–4 enumerate all permutations; include sizes 8, 9, 16, and 32 with recorded forward, reverse, rotated, and fixed shuffled permutations so representation thresholds are crossed. Include constant keys/dynamic values, dynamic keys, heterogeneous scalar and quoted collection keys, nested maps/sets/vectors, floats, generated destructuring names, duplicate subtrees, and reader/non-reader metadata. At least one dynamic expression must reach the emitter in each nonconstant case. For fixed options and admission/expansion inputs, every permutation/representation on every host MUST yield identical canonical tree bytes/root, full C1/key vectors, and per-format provenance. Compare byte content, not host array identity or printed forms. Constant cases separately assert the literal fast path.\n3. **Set representation/permutation property.** The same bounded sizes/permutations for dynamic sets; require identical roots and full relations under a fixed replay. Include nested dynamic sets and different expressions evaluating to the same value. Assert element-address ordering and retain all expression occurrences before runtime set insertion. Do not deduplicate compiled elements as an optimization.\n4. **Order, pairing, and multiplicity oracle.** Independently project each compiled key/value child and assert that the actual emitted pair-key vector is sorted by the specified comparator, that pairs remain intact, and that the seed is first. Add different source key forms that compile to the same key code with different values to exercise the value-root secondary key; add distinct forms compiling to an identical entire pair/element to exercise ties without occurrence loss. Changing only source-position or variable-node metadata MUST NOT change code order/root. Preserve semantic metadata in literal payloads. Use an independent expectation builder, not the emitter helper being tested.\n5. **Executable behavior.** Run portable fixtures through walker, semantic, stack, and register execution where those existing routes support them, on the four host lanes. Record an observable trace using the existing test harness's explicit effects/streams: key-before-value, canonical pair/element order, no duplication, and no later event after an operand failure/continuation transfer. Include dynamic keys colliding at runtime and assert the canonically last value wins; include set-value collisions. Compare pure distinct-key results with the unchanged mathematical map/set contents. Explicit `assoc`/`conj` calls, dynamic vectors, `let`, and `do` MUST retain written order; swapping their effectful arguments MUST remain observable and their ordered canonical rows MUST remain distinct. These controls prevent an invalid global sorter from passing.\n6. **Entry points and generation.** Cover `compile-form`, `compile`, both relevant `compile-program` modes, and `lower-unit`. Keep the real production macro/twin-call fixture and the complete expansion-record chain in the equality gate. Prove that pre-expansion collection normalization does not fabricate or erase expansion origins. Include compiler-generated arity maps rather than testing only hand-built user maps.\n7. **Legacy loading and identity boundaries.** Load a retained pre-fix artifact by its old address and execute it without recompilation; retain its old C1/provenance if present. Also retain the unannotated legacy-load/missing-C1-refusal tests. Replaying fixed coordinates on the new compiler must agree across hosts; admitting the same content with two different tokens must still produce different origins and occurrence keys.\n\nRecord portable generators/permutations as data or use an explicitly specified deterministic generator with recorded seeds; host `rand`, host hash iteration, and test-time self-generated goldens are not an oracle. Any unsupported host/form route must have its exact existing refusal recorded. A newly missing required portable comparison is a failed/incomplete gate, not a green skip.\n\n**Regression gate:** re-run cc4's ENTIRE strengthened `yang.clojure-callsite-test` battery on JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt, preserving the eight-member cohort, publications, negative admissions, retained-evidence negatives, and existing honest reader/interop gates. Use the r2 command forms, plus the new collection suite:\n\n```text\nclojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-callsite-test\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test --config-merge '{:ns-regexp \"^yang\\\\.clojure-callsite-test$\"}'\nbb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only yang.clojure-callsite-test\nbb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core --only '^yang.clojure-callsite-test$'\n```\n\nRun the new portable namespace through the corresponding four selectors as well; run affected compiler/manifest tests and kondo on touched files. Record exact commands, toolchains, source/test pins, executed counts, and refusal inventories. r2's 45 JVM / 40 Node-Dart test counts are historical baselines, not counts to force after adding tests. J2's four-namespace manifest refusal MUST be resolved by its owner before four-host acceptance; zero Jolt children is not a pass. Observe the repository's targeted-test/remote-lane rules and required `dao.stream` merge gate; do not launch a branch full suite as part of this ruling.\n\n**Re-pinning is REQUIRED.** This repair changes canonical executable bytes. After reviewing the new operand vectors and establishing raw four-host agreement, record one post-fix reference bundle and use that identical bundle as fixed expectations on every host. Re-record the complete cohort, including unchanged members, so root, full C1/key digests, per-format provenance, producing logs, publication/table/record/manifest addresses, and compiler revision pins remain mutually consistent. No Dart-specific expectation, no runtime recomputation of expected digests, and no origin/path/name normalization are permitted.\n\nKeep the r2 divergence file, raw logs, and findings immutable as historical defect evidence. Archive the original active snapshots/captures under an explicitly pre-fix name before replacing any active fixture; publish a before/after ledger keyed by compiler revision and input pins. Re-pinning a failing host in isolation is not a repair. A JVM address changing too is expected and is not grounds to preserve the old broken ordering.\n\nThe fix's regression result does not itself close cc4 adoption. Resume cc4's declared B/S, pre/post-port and cross-check-host budget work only after determinism gates pass. Preserve the existing finite ceiling declaration; record any exceedance honestly. Pin this ordering fix separately from Phase 2 `d60038ef`, so its additional compile-time content projection/sorting costs and changed artifact sizes are visible confounders rather than falsely attributed to the continuation port. The r2 report states those measurements were not executed. D1 remains dissolved under the dispatch; this ruling does not revive it.\n\n## RULING 4 — versioning, addresses, and legacy artifacts\n\n**Yes: there is a one-time canonical shift for affected newly compiled artifacts under this new compiler revision.** Changed collection applications change ancestor roots and may change occurrence paths, C1 keys/tables, instruction vectors/provenance, macro input/output records, and containing publication addresses. Even an application elsewhere in a changed root gets a different C1 key because the root is part of that key. There is no requirement that every artifact changes.\n\n**MUST:** advance the Clojure frontend manifest revision from the inspected `\"c1-1\"` to `\"c1-2\"` and declare its lowering profile as `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1` (if the dispatch base has already advanced, choose and record a fresh nonconflicting successor before recording expectations). Keep the C1 marker value `1` and the existing C1 support claim: identity/coverage schema has not changed. Keep SPI and UAST/execution grammar versions unchanged. The shared UAST-to-instruction lowerer has not changed for a given UAST, so its own lowering-profile stamp must not be bumped merely because this frontend now supplies different input trees. Pin the compiler commit and this frontend ordering profile with the replay fixtures.\n\nThis follows the original design's §3.1 distinction between frontend/lowering revisions and execution grammar revisions, and `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.2.1–5.2.2: content integrity, derivation verification under an exact profile, and executable loadability are distinct obligations. The existing manifest function is at `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:43–54`.\n\n**Legacy artifacts MUST continue to load at their original addresses**, subject to their already-existing content/contract/dependency checks. Loaders must not sort their operands, rehash them under the new policy, rewrite stored facts, or relabel old C1 keys as new ones. Old executable operand order remains old executable behavior. No storage rewrite or destructive migration is required. Recompilation/republishing creates new immutable artifacts and new addresses; consumers electing to upgrade update their references explicitly. Old side tables remain attached to old roots, and absent legacy provenance is not fabricated.\n\n**Address guarantee:** identical canonical bytes retain identical content addresses regardless of compiler version. Identical source text does NOT promise identical compiled addresses across unpinned compiler/profile revisions. C1 §5.2 replay equality already pins the compiler/profile revision and all other listed inputs; it never guaranteed source-to-address stability across compiler upgrades. Reproducing an old compilation requires its old pinned compiler/profile (or loading its retained output), not silently applying this new normalization. Historical evidence remains valid evidence of its own artifacts, not a certificate for newly compiled roots.\n\nAcceptance remains pending the new fix, independently checked re-pins, four-host regression results, legacy execution evidence, and the separate cc4 budget closure. This document rules policy and bounds; it does not claim those gates have passed.\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\nfc15a56c10ed2d59a842e0f677b3bcaa23ae1331112fdd3aaff13895b3d0a12b  src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
